        description:
            "Create a writable file, or replace one whole. Prefer edit_file for a change to an existing file; this refuses to overwrite a file you have not read this session.",
        props: {
            path: { type: "string", description: "File to write" },
            content: { type: "string", description: "Full new contents" },
        },
        required: ["path", "content"],
    },
]

// ── Computer-use tools ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Appended by buildToolset ONLY while the matching gate is on, so the common
// case (both off, the shipped default) pays nothing for them.
//
// Same NAMES and parameters as the MCP tools — one vocabulary for the whole
// project, so the computer-use section of the skill documents both consumers and
// a verb added to the wrapper lands in both with the same shape.
//
// The DESCRIPTIONS are re-authored rather than copied, for two reasons that both
// come from this being a conversation and not a protocol adapter:
//   1. Every byte rides on every step of every turn here, while an MCP client
//      pays for the list once per session. Hence terse — and the cross-cutting
//      rules (focus first, preference order) are stated ONCE in the system
//      prompt instead of repeated in eleven schemas.
//   2. THIS CLIENT HAS NO EYES. The MCP wording sends an agent to a screenshot
//      to read what the tree cannot show; here that would be advice to stare at
//      a PNG it cannot see. Where vision is the only answer, these say so.
const SEE_TOOL_DEFS = [
    {
        name: "query_app",
        description:
            "Look inside ANOTHER application's window through its accessibility tree: a flat list of controls with role, name, text, the actions each one exposes, and window-relative bounds. This is how you see outside Nidara — you have no other view of another app. Targets the focused window unless you pass `app`. "
            // Measured 2026-07-30: asked to read Nidara's own Settings window, the
            // model spent three steps here ("Nidara Settings", then the Hyprland
            // class) before giving up, because nothing said the inside has a
            // different door. The index line for query_ui now points back.
            + "OTHER apps only — Nidara's own windows are not in this tree under any name; read those with `run_action query_ui <selector>`. "
            + "A real window holds hundreds of controls, so pass `match` when you know what you are after; a match that finds little answers with `showing`, the labels on screen — read those before asking for a whole window. Some toolkits expose little or nothing; if the tree comes back empty, say so rather than acting blind. "
            // Measured failure, same model, twice: with an earlier query_app in its
            // history it answered about the CURRENT screen from that old copy and made
            // no call at all (steps=1 tools=0), while the answer was sitting in the
            // live screen. A file read stays true until something writes; a screen
            // read is false the instant it is taken, and nothing said so.
            + "The result is a SNAPSHOT of one instant, not a view that stays open: a screen changes on its own — a terminal scrolls, a dialog opens, a page loads. Before answering about what is on screen NOW, call this again. An earlier result in this conversation describes a screen that no longer exists, so reusing it is how you end up reporting something that is not there.",
        props: {
            app: { type: "string", description: "App name substring; omit for the focused window" },
            match: { type: "string", description: "Keep only controls whose name, text or role CONTAINS this (substring, not a regex). \"a|b\" keeps either — ask for both ends of a drag in one call" },
        },
        required: [],
    },
]

